course: dictionary
lesson: 5083
title: Insure: a word about money
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To insure something, you pay a company a small amount regularly. If the thing is lost, damaged or stolen, the company pays you money back. The clue is in the money: insure is a word about planning ahead for bad luck.
explain: Look at this sentence: «The Affordable Care Act ... helped many more people get insured.» The words around it are the clues: people who were already ill needed care, and insurance helps pay for that care. So get insured means to buy a plan that pays money if you are ill or hurt.
explain: Context helps you again. «Luckily the house was insured.» The story does not explain the plan, but it tells you the family was protected if something went wrong. Ask: what would fit here? A plan that pays for loss. Then check your meaning in the sentence to see if it still makes sense.
fact: «Insure» means to pay a company a small amount regularly, so the company pays you if something is lost, damaged or stolen.
fact: «Insured» is the past form: «The house was insured» means it was covered by a plan that would pay for loss.
fact: People insure cars, houses and phones, and some insure their health or their travel.
fact: «Insurers» are the companies that sell insurance, as in «made insurers cover people who were already ill».
fact: In the sense of making sure something happens, «ensure» is often used instead of «insure».
exercise: Mum says, «We need to insure the car before we drive it.» What does insure mean here? | *Pay a company a small amount so it pays if the car is damaged | Wash the car every week | Sell the car to a friend | Park the car on the hill
exercise: «Luckily the house was insured.» What does this tell the reader? | The house was very old | The family was out for dinner | *There was a plan to pay for the loss | The house was painted blue
exercise: Find and copy the word from «helped many more people get insured» that names the companies that sell insurance. Which word is it? | people | *insurers | many | helped
test: Which is a good reason to insure a bike? | Because you want it to be stolen so you get money | So you can ride it in the rain | *So you get money back if it is stolen or broken | To make it heavier
test: What is the past form of insure? | insuring | insurance | insures | *insured
exam: Which sentence means a person bought a plan that pays out if they are hurt? | The dog will insure the ball in the park | *Ella's dad decided to insure himself, so the hospital bills would be paid if he was hurt | We insure the sun to shine each morning | The cake was insured with extra cream
